# Cloudflare deploy credentials — P-7.
#
# Copy to `.env.cloudflare` (gitignored) and fill in. This file holds the
# credential that can deploy to production; treat it like `.dev.vars`.
#
#   cp .env.cloudflare.example .env.cloudflare
#   chmod 600 .env.cloudflare
#
# Consumed by sourcing it before a wrangler command:
#
#   set -a && . ./.env.cloudflare && set +a && npx wrangler whoami
#
# NOT a Next.js env file. Next only loads `.env`, `.env.local`,
# `.env.[NODE_ENV]` and their `.local` variants, so nothing here ever reaches
# a bundle. Wrangler likewise reads `.dev.vars`, not this. It is a shell file.
#
# ── Why a scoped token rather than `wrangler login` ──────────────────────────
# It is the same mechanism CI uses, so setting it up here exercises the real
# deploy path instead of a browser OAuth session that CI can never reproduce.
# See deployment.md §2.1.
#
# ── Creating the token ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare dashboard → My Profile → API Tokens → Create Token
#   → "Create Custom Token"
#
# Permissions (account-level, needed now):
#   Account · Workers Scripts      · Edit   — deploy the Worker
#   Account · Workers R2 Storage   · Edit   — create/manage the cache bucket
#   Account · Account Settings     · Read   — `wrangler whoami`
#
# Permissions (zone-level, add once P-8 lands and the domain is on Cloudflare):
#   Zone    · DNS                  · Edit   — custom domain record
#   Zone    · Workers Routes       · Edit   — bind the Worker to the domain
#
# Account Resources: Include → the client's account
# Zone Resources:    Include → the specific zone (only once P-8 exists)
#
# The token is shown ONCE. Losing it means minting a new one — no recovery.

CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
